{
  "_comment": [
    "App-owned RLS classification (§4.3). Gate does NOT infer table security from SQL,",
    "so every app table must be classified here or its security posture is undeclared.",
    "",
    "State: RLS is enabled and Postgres-enforced on all 23 app tables. bypassRls=false",
    "and superuser=false for the runtime role, so these policies actually filter —",
    "verified in both directions, not assumed.",
    "",
    "tenant  = org scope only. Either the row has no client of its own",
    "          (portal_source_bindings maps portals to sources; alert_deliveries records",
    "          how staff were notified), or it IS the org-level record (clients, portals).",
    "scoped  = org scope plus client scope on client_id, keyed on app.req_client_id.",
    "",
    "app.req_client_id is a custom setting because every natural name (client_id,",
    "portal_id) is reserved and refused through caller-supplied rlsContext, and the",
    "blessed alternative trustedRuntimeContext.portalId needs isolated_store.rls.delegate,",
    "which this app does not hold. The platform docs call a custom key here a reviewed",
    "temporary fallback; that is exactly its status.",
    "",
    "An ABSENT client setting grants org scope, not nothing, because a large part of the",
    "backend is legitimately cross-client: the worker claims whatever job is due, one",
    "embedding batch can span clients, and alerts are org-scoped. See 0009 for why that",
    "is defence in depth rather than a loophole."
  ],
